                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the convergence`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Vossberg just lost its largest water-utility account — <strong>EUR 43M, 6% of revenue</strong> — not on price or hardware quality, but to a rival who bundled cheap sensors with a predictive-analytics subscription. The line between hardware and software is dissolving. Diagnose <em>what is actually converging</em>: the sensors are commoditising (38% margin, eroding 2–3 points a year) while value and recurring margin (60–75%) migrate to the analytics layer. And ask the hard question — how much of today's healthy 16% EBIT is <strong>borrowed time</strong> on an installed base that will churn? Read the <em>speed</em> right, because the rest of your plan runs on the clock you set here.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Set the explore-exploit allocation`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Split the <strong>EUR 60M two-year envelope</strong> between defending the core (exploit) and building the software business (explore). You <em>cannot fully fund both</em>. Pure exploitation maximises near-term profit and organizational fit but lets the innovation pipeline decay — you optimise the thing the market is walking away from. Pure exploration builds future value but starves the cash engine that still funds 84% of the company, and exploration doesn't pay for itself yet. Set an allocation that keeps profitability viable <strong>and</strong> builds a credible pipeline, and assume a payoff horizon you can actually defend.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Redesign the reporting lines`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Adaptation fails on structure as often as on strategy. Today Digital Ventures (28 people, EUR 9M) reports <em>into</em> the hardware P&L, where its losses are a line the core manager wants to cut and it is measured on hardware metrics. <strong>Stieglitz's point: the strategy can be right and the structure still kills it.</strong> Choose where explore and exploit report — integrate, separate into an ambidextrous unit, or hybridise — and decide what the venture <strong>shares</strong> with the core (the installed base, sales relationships and calibration data that are its only edge) and what it must <em>not</em> share (the core's metrics and bonus logic). Redesign the incentives so the core doesn't quietly suffocate the new business.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Time the pivot`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Adaptation is a timing problem. Set the <em>sequence and pace</em> of the shift from hardware to subscription, and gate each escalation of investment behind milestones so you learn before you commit. Pivot too hard and you signal decline before the subscription revenue exists — your hardware customers don't rebuy on that cycle and your salesforce is paid on hardware. Pivot too late and rivals lock in the installed base. <strong>Then the test:</strong> a rival just acquired an analytics firm and the convergence window is closing faster than your roadmap assumed. Do you pull investment forward and re-base your gates, or hold a plan set for the old pace? Defend it to the board.`,

