                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_analytics:     `<strong>That's the right read.</strong> Sensors are commoditising and the value is migrating to the analytics/subscription layer — that is exactly why a software-native rival took your account with cheap hardware plus a subscription. Now the rest of your plan has to be built for where the value is going, not where it is.`,
                    coach_r1_service:       `<strong>Calibration and service is a real cash stream, but it is not where the value is migrating.</strong> Maintenance keeps the installed base warm; it does not capture the recurring analytics margin that just beat you. Don't mistake the moat around the old business for the new value pool.`,
                    coach_r1_price:         `<strong>This isn't a price war you can wait out.</strong> The rival didn't win on a discount — it won on an integrated subscription you can't match. If you read it as temporary discounting, you'll defend price while the value quietly migrates to a layer you don't compete in.`,
                    coach_r1_hardware:      `<strong>'The 16% margin proves the core is healthy' is exactly the obsolescence trap.</strong> That margin is a lagging indicator of an installed base that is starting to churn. The market is redefining your core while your reporting still says everything is fine. Read the migration, not yesterday's P&L.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named where value is migrating AND that today's EBIT is borrowed time on a base that will churn. That is the honest reading that lets you size the urgency correctly — now allocate and structure for it before the window closes.`,
                    coach_r1_complacent:    `<strong>'If it were eroding we'd already see it' is the lagging-indicator trap.</strong> By the time EBIT visibly falls, the reference accounts and the engineers will already be gone. Profit confirms the past; it does not warn you about the convergence ahead.`,
                    coach_r1_slowread:      `<strong>You've read the convergence as years away.</strong> But two more reference accounts are already evaluating the same competitor, and margin erodes every year. Set the clock too slow here and your gates, your pace and your structure will all arrive late.`,
                    coach_r2_overexploit:   `<strong>You put the bulk of the envelope into hardware cost-down and starved the future.</strong> Near-term profitability looks great — but your competitor just took your second reference account on subscription analytics, and in 18 months, with churn, where is that 16% margin? You optimised the thing the market is walking away from. What did your pipeline value do over those two years?`,
                    coach_r2_overexplore:   `<strong>Bold — but you starved the core that still funds 84% of the company.</strong> The hardware business just lost the engineers and capex it needed to hold its margin, and exploration doesn't fund itself yet. Now both businesses are bleeding. How do you keep the cash engine alive long enough for the new business to matter?`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A viable split.</strong> You kept the cash engine funded while putting real money into the pipeline — that's the only way to fund the transition out of the core's own cash flow. Now make the structure protect what you just bought.`,
                    coach_r2_immediate:     `<strong>Booking the software upside this year is how you lose the board's trust twice.</strong> Exploration pays off on a staged horizon; credit it on day one and you'll miss the number and look like you didn't understand your own bet. Credit the pipeline as milestones land.`,
                    coach_r3_separate:      `<strong>An ambidextrous structure is what lets exploration survive.</strong> A separate P&L with its own metrics and incentives protects the new business from being measured on hardware logic and cut as a cost line. Stieglitz's point made real — now make sure it still shares the assets that give it an edge.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid can work — separate P&L, shared platform and sales force.</strong> It protects the venture's metrics while keeping the coordination and the installed-base access. The risk is coordination cost and the core reasserting its gravity; watch that the shared sales force still sells the subscription.`,
                    coach_r3_integrate:     `<strong>You gave Digital Ventures more money but left it inside the hardware P&L.</strong> Its losses are a line the core manager wants to cut and its metrics are hardware metrics. The new business will be measured on the old logic and quietly strangled. The strategy can be right and the structure still kills it — where does exploration report?`,
                    coach_r3_overseparate:  `<strong>You spun the venture fully out and cut it off from its only advantage.</strong> Clean P&L, total independence — but no installed base, no sales relationships, no calibration data. Now it's a sub-scale startup competing head-on with better-funded software natives. What should it share with the core, and what should it not?`,
                    coach_r3_smother:       `<strong>Sharing everything, including the metrics and bonus pool, keeps the venture inside the core's logic.</strong> Even with its own P&L, if it is measured and paid on hardware terms the core's gravity will pull it apart. Protect its metrics, or the structure undoes the strategy.`,
                    coach_r3_lowincentive:  `<strong>You left the hardware bonuses untouched.</strong> Every senior manager is still paid to defend the core, so the venture loses every internal contest for talent and attention. Redesign the incentives or the new business is strangled by the comp plan, not the strategy.`,
                    coach_r4_aggressive:    `<strong>You announced a hard shift to subscription before the revenue exists.</strong> The market likes the story for a week — but your hardware customers don't rebuy on that cycle, your salesforce is paid on hardware, and you've just told analysts your highest-margin revenue is shrinking. Did you time the pivot to the convergence window, or to a slide?`,
                    coach_r4_tooslow:       `<strong>Too cautious — you're pivoting slower than the window is closing.</strong> A gentle revenue shift signals to rivals that the installed base is still theirs to take. Adaptation is a timing problem; arrive late and the convergence window shuts before your new business has scale.`,
                    coach_r4_measured:      `<strong>A measured, gated shift is the credible pivot.</strong> You're moving the revenue mix fast enough to matter without signalling collapse before the subscription business exists. Now let the milestones, not the headline, govern how hard you push.`,
                    coach_r4_upfront:       `<strong>Committing the whole envelope upfront throws away your ability to learn.</strong> Staged gates exist precisely so you can adjust as the convergence speed reveals itself. Bet it all now and a bad assumption becomes an irreversible loss.`,
                    coach_r4_rigid:         `<strong>The window just moved and your gates didn't.</strong> A rival's acquisition accelerated the convergence past the pace your roadmap assumed — holding the old plan rigidly means arriving late. What in your plan was supposed to tell you you were early or late, and why isn't it firing now?`,
                    coach_r4_panic:         `<strong>Ripping up the plan and dumping everything into explore is the over-exploration error under pressure.</strong> A hard 50% pivot announced overnight strains the core's cash and morale and signals decline to the market — you replaced one mistimed extreme with another. Adapt the gates; don't detonate them.`,
                    coach_r4_adaptive:      `<strong>That is adaptive timing done right.</strong> You pulled investment forward where the milestones were already met, protected the core's cash, and re-based the gates to the new pace — using new information to adjust instead of either freezing or panicking. That is the pivot that survives the board.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
