                    insight_adapted:        `Ambidextrous & adapted: you read the convergence honestly, balanced the EUR 60M so the core kept funding the transition, protected the venture in a separate structure that still shared the installed base and data, and timed the pivot to the window — adapting the gates when the rival accelerated it. The strategy and the structure told one story.`,
                    insight_trapped:        `The adaptation didn't take. Either the venture was strangled inside the core's logic, cut off from its only edge, or the cash engine was starved before the new business could pay its way. As Stieglitz warns, the structure decided the outcome as much as the strategy did.`,
                    insight_short:          `A partial adaptation. The pieces are there but they don't yet reinforce each other — the allocation, the structure and the pivot timing are pulling on slightly different clocks, and the convergence window doesn't wait for them to align.`,
                    insight_profit_low:     `Profitability fell below a viable line: either you starved the core that funds 84% of the company, or you pivoted hard on revenue mix before the subscription business existed. The core has to fund the transition — it can't fund it dead.`,
                    insight_profit_strong:  `Strong profitability held: you defended enough of the core's margin to keep funding the explore bets out of the company's own cash flow, which is the only sustainable way to pay for a multi-year pivot.`,
                    insight_strangled:      `The venture was strangled by structure. Left inside the hardware P&L and measured on hardware metrics, it was always going to be cut as a cost line — exactly the failure mode Stieglitz describes, where the right strategy dies of the wrong reporting line.`,
                    insight_overseparated:  `The venture was cut adrift. Full independence stripped it of the installed base, the sales relationships and the calibration data that were its only edge over better-funded software natives — separation protected its metrics but destroyed its advantage.`,
                    insight_starved:        `The cash engine stalled. Over-investing in explore — or panicking into a hard pivot — pulled the capex and engineers the core needed to hold its margin, and exploration couldn't yet fund itself. Both businesses ended up bleeding.`,
                    insight_balanced:       `Pipeline, fit and adaptation speed all finished healthy — the rare balanced outcome where the new business has credible value, the structure lets it survive, and the pace matches the closing window.`,

                    hint_starved:           `You've starved the core that funds 84% of the company. Restore enough exploit spend to hold the margin — exploration can't fund itself yet, and a dead cash engine funds nothing.`,
                    hint_strangled:         `The venture is being strangled inside the hardware P&L. Move it to a separate or hybrid structure with its own metrics, or the core's logic will cut it as a cost line.`,
                    hint_overseparated:     `You've cut the venture off from the installed base and data that were its only edge. Have it share the core's assets while keeping its own metrics — separation should protect, not isolate.`,
                    hint_profit:            `Profitability is below a viable line. Re-check the allocation and the pivot pace: the core has to keep funding the transition, so don't starve it or signal decline before the subscription revenue exists.`,
                    hint_pipeline:          `Innovation-pipeline value is dangerously low — you're optimising a converging core. Shift more of the envelope into the analytics platform and credit it on a staged horizon.`,
                    hint_speed:             `Adaptation speed is lagging the window. Pivot the revenue mix faster and gate the investment to milestones so you can move as the convergence accelerates.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — pipeline, fit and adaptation speed all healthy. Hold the ambidextrous structure and let the milestones, not a bold headline, govern the pivot.`,

